    "math_academy_id": "q-46831",
    "knowledge_point_id": "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75",
    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator",
    "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4}.$",
    "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2+2}-2}{{2}^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-2}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+2}-2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x+2})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x+2-4}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{1}{(x+2)(\\sqrt{x+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2+2)(\\sqrt{2+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
